---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Figure from "../components/Figure.astro";
import Snippet from "../components/Snippet.astro";
import PageHeader from "../components/PageHeader.astro";
import DocShell from "../components/DocShell.astro";
import DocSection from "../components/DocSection.astro";
import { melactDune } from "../data/samples";

const toc = [
  { id: "switch", label: "Switch two lines" },
  { id: "works", label: "What works" },
  { id: "underneath", label: "What changes underneath" },
  { id: "rules", label: "Rules that matter" },
  { id: "server", label: "Server rendering" },
  { id: "checked", label: "How it is checked" },
  { id: "limits", label: "Not yet, and limits" },
  { id: "not-goals", label: "Not goals" },
];

const works = [
  { group: "Runtime", items: ["View", "Template", "Reconcile", "delegated events"] },
  {
    group: "Hooks",
    items: ["useState", "useReducer", "useEffect*", "useLayoutEffect*", "useMemo*", "useCallback*", "useRef", "useImperativeHandle*", "useId"],
  },
  {
    group: "React",
    items: ["components", "context", "React.memo", "React.act", "React.Event (every family)", "React.createElement with makeProps"],
  },
  { group: "ReactDOM", items: ["ReactDOM.Client", "ReactDOM.Style", "ReactDOM.Ref", "ReactDOMServer.renderToString", "ReactDOM.Client.hydrateRoot"] },
  { group: "Routing", items: ["ReasonReactRouter"] },
  { group: "JSX", items: ["Reason", "OCaml [@JSX]", "mlx"] },
];

const notGoals = [
  { title: "npm React components.", text: "react-query and other packages from npm will not work. Only OCaml reason-react code is the target." },
  { title: "Suspense, concurrent rendering, useTransition semantics.", text: "useTransition and startTransition run synchronously." },
  { title: "Server components.", text: "" },
  { title: "Signals.", text: "There is no reactive graph. A component function runs again, and its holes are compared." },
];
---

<Base title="Migrating from reason-react to Melact" description="What changes, what stays, and how Melact is checked against React 18 on real reason-react apps.">
  <PageHeader eyebrow="Migrating from reason-react" title="Change two lines. Keep everything else.">
    Melact keeps reason-react's surface (<code>React.useState</code>, <code>useEffect</code>, <code>useContext</code>, <code>ReactDOM.Client</code>,
    <code>React.Event</code>, <code>ReasonReactRouter</code> …) and replaces what is underneath it. Pure-OCaml reason-react code is the compatibility target.
  </PageHeader>

  <DocShell toc={toc}>
    <DocSection id="switch" eyebrow="1" title="Switch two lines in dune">
      <Snippet code={melactDune} lang="diff" title="dune" />
      <p>Your components stay as they are: the same JSX in mlx, Reason or OCaml <code>[@JSX]</code>, and the same hooks with the same signatures.</p>
    </DocSection>

    <DocSection id="works" eyebrow="2" title="What works">
      <div class="not-prose grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
        {
          works.map((group) => (
            <div class="card p-5">
              <p class="eyebrow">{group.group}</p>
              <ul class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap gap-1.5">
                {group.items.map((item) => (
                  <li class="rounded-md border border-line bg-bg px-2 py-0.5 font-mono text-[0.76rem] text-ink-2">{item}</li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <p>
        Every hook with a key is supported. Every <code>React.Event</code> family reads the native event; handlers see the <code>currentTarget</code> they were set on,
        as in React. Delegated mouse events skip the handlers of a disabled <code>button</code>, <code>input</code>, <code>select</code> or <code>textarea</code> and go
        on bubbling, as React does.
      </p>
    </DocSection>

    <DocSection id="underneath" eyebrow="3" title="What changes underneath">
      <Figure
        name="surface"
        alt="What melact-ppx and the Melact library make of unchanged reason-react code: a JSX component is a direct call to make; React.createElement with makeProps is the same call with a key; React.Event accessors read the native event, delegated to document; each hook call gets a typed slot key; a component without hooks is a pure view with no instance; ReactDOM.Client clones templates into the element; renderToString and hydrateRoot are Served.render and Served.hydrate; ReasonReactRouter.useUrl reads the location on popstate"
        caption="Each line of reason-react code on the left compiles, unchanged, to what is on the right."
      />
      <p>
        <code>melact-ppx</code> writes <code>makeProps</code> next to every <code>[@react.component]</code>, so <code>React.createElement Foo.make (Foo.makeProps ~label
        ())</code> is <code>make ~label ()</code> with a key, checked by the type checker, and no props object exists at run time.
      </p>
    </DocSection>

    <DocSection id="rules" eyebrow="4" title="Rules that matter">
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Render purely.</strong> Melact relies on the Rules of React: rendering is a pure function of props, state and context. React Compiler relies on the
          same contract.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Do not mutate in place.</strong> Change detection is physical equality. Code that mutates an array or a record in place and passes the same
          reference back will not re-render that part.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Hooks at the top level.</strong> A hook inside an <code>if</code>, a <code>match</code>, a loop or a callback raises the <code>hook</code> alert at
          build time. It is a warning, not an error, so reason-react code that bends the rule still compiles.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A key resets.</strong> A child with a <code>key</code> is mounted again when the key changes, as in React, which is how React code resets a
          component.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </DocSection>

    <DocSection id="server" eyebrow="5" title="Server rendering">
      <p>
        <code>ReactDOMServer.renderToString</code> writes HTML in React's format, so React's own and server-reason-react's HTML hydrate too, and a Melact app renders
        natively with <a href="https://github.com/ml-in-barcelona/server-reason-react">server-reason-react</a>. Hydration code is compiled under
        <code>-hydratable</code>. <code>useId</code> stays a client counter: Melact's hydration writes every attribute from the client render, so ids in
        <code>id</code>, <code>htmlFor</code> and <code>aria-*</code> agree on the page whatever format the server used.
      </p>
    </DocSection>

    <DocSection id="checked" eyebrow="6" title="How it is checked">
      <div class="not-prose grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="card p-6">
          <p class="font-mono text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink tabular-nums">33/33</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-ink-2">steps of reason-react's own demo with the same DOM under React 18 and Melact, compared after every click.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card p-6">
          <p class="font-mono text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink tabular-nums">605/605</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-ink-2">steps of five random walks through reason-seoul's TodoMVC: clicks, typing, submits and route changes.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        <code>compat/run.sh</code> clones reason-react, melange-opam-template and reason-seoul's TodoMVC, changes two lines in their <code>dune</code> files and builds
        them against Melact. Then it renders reason-react's demo with React and with Melact and compares the DOM after every click, and walks the TodoMVC randomly
        comparing the DOM after every step.
      </p>
      <p>
        The TodoMVC was written for reason-react 0.9, so both copies first get the same upgrade to reason-react 0.17 (<code>ReactEvent.</code> becomes
        <code>React.Event.</code>, and <code>Index.re</code> uses <code>ReactDOM.Client</code>). One demo component binds React's npm <code>useReducer</code> directly,
        which is a non-goal, so the script removes it.
      </p>
    </DocSection>

    <DocSection id="limits" eyebrow="7" title="Not yet, and limits">
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Not yet:</strong> <code>React.createElementVariadic</code>, code typed against <code>React.component('props)</code>, Suspense on the server.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Limits:</strong> a component or custom hook that is polymorphic in its state type gets a weak type for its hook key; annotate it, or keep the state
          type concrete.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </DocSection>

    <DocSection id="not-goals" eyebrow="8" title="Not goals">
      <ul>
        {notGoals.map((item) => <li><strong>{item.title}</strong> {item.text}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </DocSection>
  </DocShell>
</Base>
